  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nLatest look at 18Z obs show some areas down around 20 percent \nhumidity scattered about the CWA, although wind gusts are still \nbelow 25 mph for much of the area. Will continue to monitor winds/RH \nthis afternoon as there may be some areas flirting with red flag \ncriteria. Due to the marginality of this, still no plans to issue an \nRFW for the afternoon.\n\nWeak cold front still on track to move south through the CWA this \nevening. Surface high pressure will build in behind the front with \nlight winds overnight. Pretty decent radiational cooling night \nsetting up again, although models do show some degree of high \ncloudiness overnight. Given the poor track record of NBM low temps \nrecently, decided to blend in about 50 percent of NBM10 numbers, \nthen even taking a few more degrees off of that in the James valley. \nAll that said, this puts lows in the 40s for most areas, but \nwouldn't be surprised if a few upper 30s sneak in once again. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front does not usher in much in the way of \ncold air, but the air mass is slightly cooler than what's on top of \nus today. Highs still expected to be in the 70s on Wednesday, likely \nflirting with 80 for some locations as well. RH values are forecast \nto drop to between 25 and 35 percent for much of the CWA, but this \nis with straight-up NBM dewpoints. Could see a scenario again where \ndewpoints perhaps drop lower than forecast, which may put some areas \ncloser to 20 percent RH. Wind gusts on Wednesday look to be \napproaching 25 mph for parts of the northern CWA at least, so fire \ndanger and RFW criteria will be something to pay attention to.\n\nWill then be watching the next push of warm air with a passing warm \nfront on Saturday. 925/850mb temps around +25C with good southerly \nmixing winds. Grand Ensemble showing the 850mb temps this Saturday \nis as high as the 95th-99th percentile. Inherited NBM highs on \nSaturday are generally in the low to mid 80s, with some upper 80s \nover the southwest CWA. Would not be surprised if these temps are \nstill too cool. NBM90th temps are more into the mid 80s to low 90s \nover the CWA, which could end up being the reality under the right \nmixing conditions with mostly sunny skies.",
